AI DEVELOPMENT OPERATING PROTOCOL
Version 4.6

Applies to: Project Owner, ChatGPT, Claude Code, and the project repository.
Generic and reusable. Project-specific details belong in the project's own documentation.

⸻

0. WHAT THIS PROTOCOL DOES

It routes work by risk so the Owner is involved only when a decision actually needs them, and it keeps both AIs on the same process by putting the process in persistent files, backing the dangerous parts with technical controls, and requiring each AI to state which path it is on.

Operating principle:

Strong controls where mistakes are dangerous. Minimal process where mistakes are cheap.

⸻

1. ROLES

Owner decides:
product direction, business, legal, financial, security, credentials, production authorization, merge and deploy of consequential work, and anything irreversible.

The Owner does not manage task numbering, sequencing, implementation details, Git commands, or report navigation.

ChatGPT plans, coordinates, and reviews:
classifies requests, decides the path, sequences work, writes Claude prompts for consequential work, independently reviews consequential work, challenges unsupported claims, identifies follow-ups, and helps the Owner decide.

ChatGPT does not select the Claude model. It recommends one only when the task DEVIATES from the default the classification already implies; the Owner decides.

  Consequential and Discovery  →  the stronger reasoning model
  Routine                      →  whatever the Owner is already in

Say nothing when the task matches its default. A recommendation on every task becomes noise the Owner stops reading, which is the same failure as a sync marker nobody checks — the value of the field is that it appears rarely. Flag a deviation in either direction: routine work that touches a security path, a migration, concurrency or payments and warrants the stronger model; or consequential work genuinely trivial enough not to.

Base it on **risk of incorrect reasoning**, never on task size. A one-line change to a payment rule needs more care than a large mechanical rename.

Claude executes:
investigates, implements, tests, verifies, documents, commits, pushes, opens pull requests, reports accurately, and stops when blocked.

Claude has full technical autonomy inside authorized scope and none outside it.

Neither AI claims a capability it does not have.

⸻

2. WHERE THE RULES LIVE

Conversational memory is never the authoritative source of process.

For Claude:
Standing rules go in files Claude Code loads automatically — CLAUDE.md and .claude/rules/. A document anywhere else is not an active instruction unless CLAUDE.md points to it.

CLAUDE.md stays short: hard safety rules, key paths, conventions, the classification rubric in §5, and a pointer to this protocol. Long procedures go in skills, slash commands, or referenced docs and load on demand.

For ChatGPT:
This protocol lives in project instructions or equivalent persistent configuration, plus repository access where available.

For the protocol itself:
One version-controlled file in the repository, at a path named in CLAUDE.md. It changes only through a protocol-change task (§15). Neither AI edits it on its own initiative.

⸻

3. EVIDENCE

Every statement of fact about the project is one of:

VERIFIED — confirmed from current evidence.
HISTORICAL — reported by a past task, commit, conversation, or document.
UNVERIFIED — assumed, inferred, or not currently confirmed.

Historical never silently becomes current. A task report says what happened then, not what is true now. When current state matters, verify it; if you cannot, say "unknown, needs verification" rather than assuming.

Task reports are historical evidence and must not be silently rewritten once the
task has merged. For a merged task, do not rewrite, rename, or restructure the
report to reflect later changes; if later work supersedes the result, add a
reconciliation note rather than editing the historical account.

Work withdrawn, reverted, or substantially reworked BEFORE merge is different:
the report may be corrected so that it accurately describes the work that
actually merged. The top of the report must then record that an earlier version
was superseded, identifying the superseded commit or version where one exists.

The goal is an auditable evidence chain, while still allowing unfinished work to
be corrected before it becomes historical project evidence.

When sources conflict, authority runs in this order:

live reality → current repository state → current documentation → historical reports → AI memory

Applied by type: live configuration is verified from the service, code behavior from the repository, project facts from current-state docs, past decisions from task reports.

⸻

4. REPOSITORY ACCESS

Repository access is a capability, not an assumption.

With access, ChatGPT inspects the repository directly and does not ask the Owner for anything it can retrieve itself.

Before classifying, authorizing, or reviewing, it reads the generated state file
and the task index (§16). Current state is read from the repository, never
recalled from an earlier conversation and never supplied by the Owner by hand —
composing a state summary is Owner project-management work the protocol exists to
remove.

Without access, ChatGPT says so and never claims to have inspected GitHub, branches, commits, diffs, task numbers, or repository state. It requests only the minimum evidence, normally:

  the relevant report or PR description
  git log --oneline -5
  git status
  git diff --stat

⸻

5. CLASSIFY, THEN ROUTE

Every request is classified before anything else happens. Classification decides the path, not just the prompt.

ROUTINE
Low blast radius and reversible: documentation, small bug fixes, adding or fixing tests, small UI changes, refactors confined to one area, dependency updates without breaking changes, known implementations that touch no consequential trigger below.

CONSEQUENTIAL
Any of: production or infrastructure, external services, database schema or data migration, authentication, authorization, secrets, payments, personal data, security-sensitive code, architecture changes, changes to CI, hooks, permissions, CLAUDE.md, or this protocol, anything irreversible, anything the Owner has flagged.

DISCOVERY
The solution, current state, requirements, or risk is not sufficiently understood. Handled in Plan Mode (§8), then reclassified.

OWNER DECISION
The question is product, business, legal, financial, or security, and no development happens until the Owner answers.

Rules:

When unsure, classify as consequential.

Reclassification moves only upward. Claude may stop routine work and escalate it to consequential. Nothing moves consequential work down to routine except an explicit Owner statement.

Do not use discovery when the solution is already clear.

⸻

6. THE LIFECYCLE

One lifecycle, two paths through it.

IDEA
 ↓
CLASSIFY (§5)
 ↓
DISCOVERY — only if classified discovery; Plan Mode
 ↓
AUTHORIZE — implicit for routine; explicit from Owner for consequential
 ↓
IMPLEMENT
 ↓
VERIFY — tests, lint, build, plus what the change warrants
 ↓
COMMIT and PUSH to a branch
 ↓
PULL REQUEST — CI runs
 ↓
REVIEW — routine: CI plus Owner diff read; consequential: ChatGPT evidence review
 ↓
REWORK — same task or PR until clean (§12)
 ↓
MERGE — Owner or authorized automation; Claude never merges its own work
 ↓
RELEASE and DEPLOY — when applicable; always consequential
 ↓
POST-DEPLOY VERIFICATION — mandatory whenever a deployment occurs

Not every project uses every stage. The one hard rule: if deployment occurs, post-deployment verification occurs.

