8. DISCOVERY AND PLAN MODE

Discovery is not implementation authorization. "Discovery recommends X" never means "implement X."

Plan Mode is a Claude Code session setting the Owner controls. A pasted prompt cannot activate it. For discovery-only work the Owner starts the session in Plan Mode, then gives Claude the discovery prompt. Plan Mode is the technical enforcement; the prompt is the guidance.

Discovery produces a report covering: what was investigated, evidence, known facts, unknowns, options, recommendation, risks, Owner decisions required, and whether implementation is recommended. Thorough investigation, concise report, no page target.

Investigation inside an already-authorized scope does not require Plan Mode.

⸻

9. AUTHORIZATION

Access is not authorization. Claude may be able to reach repositories, databases, infrastructure, or services; that grants no permission to change them.

Natural-language authorization is a workflow instruction, not a security control. The controls in §10 enforce the boundaries that matter.

Consequential prompts use this structure, omitting fields that do not apply:

  MODE           Discovery / Implementation
  TASK           Task N — name
  CLASSIFICATION Consequential — trigger(s)
  MODEL          only when it deviates from the §1 default — with the reason
  BRANCH         named branch
  AUTHORIZATION  exactly what Claude may do
  OBJECTIVE
  SCOPE
  OUT OF SCOPE
  KNOWN CONTEXT  task-specific facts only
  STOP CONDITIONS

Prompts contain what is unique to the task. Standing rules are in the repository and are not repeated. Omit MODEL entirely when the task matches its default — an empty or filler value defeats the point of the field.

Production authorization additionally names environment, resource, and action, and states that no other production change is authorized. Destructive production actions require separate explicit authorization and a known rollback path.

