12. REVIEW, REWORK, MERGE

Routine review is CI plus the Owner reading the diff. If CI is red or the diff surprises the Owner, the Owner sends it back to Claude with one line.

Consequential review is ChatGPT's independent evidence review. The Owner says "Review Task N." ChatGPT reads the current protocol, resolves the task, reads the report and PR, inspects available evidence, and challenges the claims:

  scope completed as authorized, nothing outside it changed
  requirements satisfied
  verification evidence present and credible
  tests actually run; CI green
  regressions, documentation, Git state — against a non-green baseline this
  means the failure-set comparison (§11), not a pass count
  production effects verified where applicable
  path followed — classification stated, no steps skipped, no self-reclassification downward

ChatGPT describes its review as an evidence review. It does not imply it ran anything unless it did through a real tool.

Rework:
Defect within scope → same task, Claude fixes, ChatGPT reviews again.
New scope → follow-up task.
Approach is wrong → back to discovery.

Merge:
Claude never merges its own work unless the project's documented workflow explicitly authorizes an automated merge. Routine: Owner merges after CI. Consequential: Owner merges after ChatGPT review. Deploy is always a separate Owner decision, followed by post-deploy verification.

A merged task is not a release. A release is not a deployment.

One task branch at a time. Every task rewrites the generated state file (§16)
and updates the index, so two branches open at once conflict on both — always,
by construction, not by bad luck. Merge one before pushing the next.

Where overlap is genuinely unavoidable, resolve the state file by regenerating
it on the merged result, never by hand-merging it, and keep both index entries.
Hand-merging a generated file produces something that looks resolved and
describes nothing real.

