13. REPORTS AND HANDOFFS

Routine: the PR description is the report. It states what changed, why, and the verification evidence.

Consequential: a task report in the repository is the durable record: what changed, why, decisions, verification evidence, documentation changes, commit, branch, concerns, follow-ups. It is historical, not current state.

THE HANDOFF BLOCK

This is the single definition of the handoff. A project's own documentation
points here; it does not keep a second copy. Two copies of one process artifact
is how the classification field went missing for six tasks — the copy that was
followed was the copy that lacked it.

The Owner is the courier between two AIs and should never have to compose
anything. Every handoff therefore ends with a ready-to-paste prompt for the
other side, when one is needed.

This applies to ALL content the Owner carries between the two AIs, in either
direction — not only the task-completion handoff. Prompts, research questions,
planning and review requests, implementation requests, protocol-change requests,
and corrections to any earlier request are all delivered as one complete,
self-contained, copy-paste-ready block:

  the Owner can copy the whole block in one action;
  it depends on nothing in the surrounding conversation;
  it is never split across blocks or messages;
  the Owner never assembles, edits, reorders, or selects pieces of it;
  a correction is never a diff or a "replace point X with this" — reissue the
    complete replacement block instead;
  placeholders requiring Owner editing are avoided, and where one is genuinely
    unavoidable it is marked unmistakably and the reason it cannot be resolved
    by the AI is stated.

This extends the courier principle already stated above; it is not a second
communication system.

The handoff is a **chat-only coordination artifact**. It is not written into the
task report and does not replace it: the report is the durable historical
record, the handoff is the interface between two AIs at one moment. Putting the
block in the report conflates the two, and a report is not where a reviewer
looks for an instruction addressed to them.

The canonical handoff carries these fields, and a block missing any of them is
incomplete: **Task**, **Classification**, **Model**, **Result**, a concise
change-and-verification sentence, **Report**, **Branch**, **Owner action**, and
a copy/paste **Prompt for ChatGPT**. The templates below are those fields in
order; they are the definition, not an illustration of one.

Claude, at the end of a consequential or discovery task:

  Task <N> — <Task Name>
  Classification: <ROUTINE | CONSEQUENTIAL | DISCOVERY>
  Model: <the model that did the work>
  Result: <complete | discovery complete | blocked>

  <One to three sentences: what changed, and how it was verified.>

  Report: <docs/tasks/TASK-<N>/completion-report.md>
  Branch: <branch>
  Owner action required: <YES — n items (see report) | NO>

  Prompt for ChatGPT — copy from here:
  Review Task <N> — <Task Name>.
  Report: <path>
  Branch: <branch> @ <commit>
  Review the AI Development Workflow documentation and the report on GitHub.

For discovery the paste block ends instead: "Awaiting approval — not
authorization to implement."

When no ChatGPT involvement is needed the field says so rather than inventing
filler: "Prompt for ChatGPT: none needed — routine, merge after CI."

ChatGPT, at the end of a classification, authorization, or review:

  Task <N> — <classification | authorization | review>
  Synced: STATE @ Task <n> · <branch> @ <commit> · CI <status>
  Verdict: <approved | rework needed | authorize | do not authorize>
  Merge: <YES | NO — what must happen first>

  <One to three sentences.>

  Prompt for Claude — copy from here:
  <complete instruction: task number, scope, authorization level, stop conditions>

The Merge field exists because "approved" was being read as merge
authorization, and the two are not the same: work can be approved as far as it
has gone while still changing. On an implementation task, YES means ChatGPT has
determined the task is complete and the Owner may merge; NO means the Owner must
not merge yet, and the reason and the action required are stated. Never rely on
"Verdict: approved" to imply it.

On a discovery task the field never implies authorization to implement. The
discovery closing line above remains authoritative.

ROUTINE WORK GETS NO HANDOFF BLOCK. Its path is one request and one merge (§7),
the PR description is the report, and a block for a CSS fix is ceremony the
Owner has to read past. Claude states the classification in the chat and gives
the PR link. This is a stated exception, not a judgment call — leaving it to
judgment is what produced the drift this section exists to correct.

Rules:

  Give the real report path. This line is what tells ChatGPT which file to
  open; a wrong or missing path is the failure the block exists to prevent.

  State the model that actually did the work, not the one that was
  recommended. Unlike the recommendation in §9 this is never omitted: it is a
  durable record, and it is the only one of the three model rules that
  survives the conversation. Reviewing a security change months later, knowing
  which model implemented it changes how hard a reader looks. That provenance
  is otherwise lost the moment the session ends.

  If the work was deliberately not committed or pushed, the Branch line must
  say so. ChatGPT reads from the repository, so a normal-looking branch line
  sends it after a file that is not there.

  "Owner action required" is a factual count, not a courtesy. Anything the
  Owner must do outside the repository counts.

ChatGPT opens every classification, authorization, and review with one line
naming what it synced from:

  Synced: STATE @ Task <n> · <branch> @ <commit> · CI <status>

One line, from the state file it just read. A missing or out-of-date marker is
itself a review finding (§14).

WHAT ENFORCEMENT CAN AND CANNOT VERIFY

Parts of this section are machine-checkable and parts are not, and the
difference is not a matter of effort. Stating it here stops a passing check
from being read as proof the process was followed.

A repository check can verify:

  - that only this section defines the handoff, and no second copy has appeared
    in another document;
  - that no document points at a handoff definition that no longer exists;
  - that this section still carries its required fields, the discovery closing
    line, and the unpushed-branch rule;
  - that a secondary document has not reintroduced an approval path that omits
    ChatGPT's planning and review role;
  - durable task-artifact conventions that are explicitly defined, such as a
    task directory containing a report.

A repository check can NOT verify:

  - that a ChatGPT conversation occurred at all;
  - that ChatGPT actually planned or independently reviewed the work;
  - that the Owner genuinely authorized it rather than waving it through;
  - that a human read or acted on the handoff;
  - that Claude's reasoning or classification was honest.

Those are human review responsibilities and remain so. **A green check means
the documentation has not drifted. It never means the workflow was followed.**
Claiming otherwise is itself a review finding.

These checks are a ratchet against documentation drift, not a merge gate.
Branch protection stays disabled (§10); a red run informs and never blocks.

In this repository they live in `tests/workflowDocumentation.test.ts` and run in
the existing test job. The check enforces this section; it does not define it —
§13 remains the only authority on what the handoff contains (§15).

