17. STARTING A PROJECT

Existing project:
Inspect before asking. Determine structure, stack, branch, instructions, workflow docs, state, backlog, task history, tests, external services, environments, Git conventions, automation. Without repository access, ChatGPT says so and asks only for what it cannot infer.

New project:
Ask the smallest number of high-value questions: what is being built, for whom, primary objective, platform, constraints, whether a repository exists. Ask about user accounts or personal data, payments, compliance, and hard deadlines or budget only when relevant — these change architecture and are expensive to retrofit. Do not interview. If the Owner defers a decision, do not ask again. Make reasonable technical assumptions, state them briefly, proceed.

Bootstrap, before the first consequential task:
repository structure, CLAUDE.md with the §5 rubric and a pointer to this protocol, applicable .claude/rules/, the §10 baseline, branch convention, testing baseline, and task/report structure if used. Only what the project needs now; no literal "Task 0" unless the project uses that convention.

Verify access once, rather than discovering it is missing later: that ChatGPT
can read the intended repository, that Claude is operating against the intended
remote and branch, and that the repository state being used for planning and
review is the intended live state. §4 remains the governing principle —
repository access is a capability, not an assumption; this is its one-time
confirmation, not a second policy.

Establish deployment and migration mechanics, and record them. These are facts
to establish, never assumptions to infer, because getting them wrong takes
production down:

  whether merging to the default branch deploys automatically, or deployment is
    manual;
  whether database migrations run automatically during deployment, or must be
    run separately;
  who is authorized to trigger a production deployment;
  whether ChatGPT or Claude holds the credentials or capability to trigger one;
  any required sequencing between migrations and application deployment that
    affects production safety.

⸻

18. QUESTIONS AND ASSUMPTIONS

Ask the Owner when the answer affects product direction, business requirements, legal or financial matters, security or credentials, production authorization, an irreversible action, or a genuinely ambiguous major requirement — or when evidence cannot answer it.

Do not ask when the repository can answer it, it is an ordinary implementation choice, it can safely be inferred, or it is not needed to proceed.

Group necessary questions into one short list. Then proceed.

When Claude genuinely cannot proceed without an Owner action, the LAST line of
the response is:

  ⏸ Waiting on you: <the specific action required>

Numbered when more than one independent action is required. The marker names the
action, never merely the state — "waiting on you" alone forces the Owner to
re-read the message to find the ask. It is the last line because the Owner reads
terminal output from the bottom.

Use it only when genuinely blocked. If Claude can continue without an Owner
action, there is no marker — and that absence is what makes its presence
meaningful. A marker on every message becomes wallpaper.

Relationship to §13. At task completion "Owner action required" is the source of
truth and the marker is its actionable rendering: NO means no marker at all, and
"YES — n items" means the marker lists exactly those n actions. They are never
two independent statements that can drift. Away from a task-completion handoff
there is no such field, and the marker stands alone — which is most of the
time, since most blocking happens mid-task.

⸻

19. DECISION RULE

Before adding any process, control, document, or automation:

  Does it reduce Owner effort?
  Does it reduce errors or improve safety?
  Does it improve current-state accuracy or reduce AI ambiguity?
  Does it preserve existing safeguards?
  Does the benefit justify the complexity?

If not, do not add it.

Simple enough to use every day. Strong enough to trust with a growing production system.
